the commands they document now live; and gs/state.md gains cmd/gs alongside internal/workroom because the page documents a CLI verb whose fold semantics and command surface sit in two areas.\n\nBASELINE AUDIT. All eight removed rows carry the \"comma-joined pseudo-path\" reason; no whole-repository, branch-name, or absolute-path row was touched, so no unrelated known-failing entry was removed to turn a red gate green. Each removed row's page list is fully contained in the ten pages this head repairs, so no removed row is still failing on a page left behind. This is checked by the gate itself rather than by my reading: CompareBaseline is bidirectional — a citation still failing but no longer recorded surfaces as \"newly\", a recorded citation that has stopped failing surfaces as \"fixed\", a changed reason surfaces as \"changed\", and a known citation that spread to an uncovered page surfaces too. A baseline edit therefore cannot hide a genuine failure in either direction, and internal/docset passes at this head. The file's own header states the same invariant, that the list may only shrink and the repairing head must delete its line in the same commit, and this head honours it exactly.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers per docs/reference/architecture.md: none in contract. The ten pages are layer 7 documentation, and the change is confined to their rests_on provenance, which is a citation graph fact rather than a behavioural interface; no exported symbol, wire shape, schema, or CLI surface moves. citation_baseline.txt is test data for the layer 7 documentation gate in internal/docset, and shrinking it is precisely the use the gate documents for it, not a relaxation of the gate. The new anchors respect layer ownership: internal/workroom (layer 5, the application interpreter and fold) for the record and glossary concepts, cmd/gs and cmd/gitseq-mcp (layer 7) for command and MCP reference pages, and internal/kernel (layer 2) for the event identifier format, which is right